<GuideLayout
  title="OEM vs ODM vs Private Label Activewear: Which Manufacturing Model Should You Choose?"
  type="Comparison Center"
  description="Compare OEM, ODM and private label activewear manufacturing by control, MOQ, speed, cost, product uniqueness and buyer risk."
>
  <p>Choosing between OEM, ODM and private label activewear is one of the first manufacturing decisions a brand must make. The right model depends on whether you need speed, product uniqueness, lower MOQ, or full control over fit, fabric and pattern development.</p>

  <h2>Quick Answer</h2>
  <p>Private label is best for fast market testing. ODM is best when you want factory-developed designs with some customization. OEM is best when your brand already owns the design, tech pack, fabric direction and fit requirements.</p>

  <h2>Manufacturing Model Comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Model</th><th>Best For</th><th>Buyer Control</th><th>Typical Risk</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Private Label</td><td>Launch testing, boutique drops, low MOQ programs</td><td>Logo, labels, packaging, selected colors</td><td>Limited exclusivity if stock styles are used</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ODM</td><td>Brands that need design support and proven silhouettes</td><td>Style selection, trim details, fabric options</td><td>Need clear approval on patterns and materials</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OEM</td><td>Established brands with tech packs and repeat volume</td><td>Full pattern, fabric, fit and construction control</td><td>Longer development cycle and higher sampling cost</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When Private Label Makes Sense</h2>
  <p>Private label activewear works well when a brand needs to validate demand before committing to full custom development. Buyers can choose existing leggings, sports bras, yoga sets or gym wear styles, then add heat transfer logos, woven labels, hangtags and packaging.</p>

  <h2>When ODM Makes Sense</h2>
  <p>ODM is useful when you need more product differentiation than stock styles but do not want to develop every detail from zero. A factory can recommend silhouettes, seam placement, fabric options and trims based on previous production knowledge.</p>

  <h2>When OEM Makes Sense</h2>
  <p>OEM is the strongest model for established activewear brands that need exclusive products. It requires clearer documents: measurement specs, grading rules, fabric standards, logo placement, color references, packaging details and quality tolerances.</p>

